translation:
Target is the proposition asserting that for every type Fm of sentences equipped with propositional connectives (🡒, ⋏, ⋎, ∼, ⊤, ⊥, with 🡘 abbreviating mutual implication), every type S of proof systems with a provability relation ⊢ on Fm, every proof system 𝓢 in S that is classically complete for propositional tautologies and closed under modus ponens (with no further assumptions such as consistency), every choice of three arbitrary functions T, K, D : Fm → Fm, and every choice of six arbitrary sentences σ, S₀, S', G, P, Q in Fm, if all of the following nine hypotheses hold:
1. (Def6 𝓢 D T) for every sentence φ in Fm, 𝓢 proves D φ 🡒 (T φ 🡘 φ) (that is, D φ 🡒 ((T φ 🡒 φ) ⋏ (φ 🡒 T φ)));
2. (Def5 𝓢 D K) as a meta-level rule, for every sentence φ in Fm, if 𝓢 proves φ and 𝓢 proves D φ, then 𝓢 proves K φ;
3. 𝓢 proves D σ;
4. 𝓢 proves D (σ 🡒 G);
5. 𝓢 proves σ 🡒 S₀;
6. 𝓢 proves S₀ 🡒 (T σ 🡒 S');
7. 𝓢 proves S' 🡒 G;
8. 𝓢 proves S' 🡒 ∼P;
9. 𝓢 proves Q 🡒 P;
then 𝓢 proves the object-level conjunction (K (σ 🡒 G)) ⋏ (σ 🡒 ∼Q). No other properties of 𝓢, T, K, or D (such as consistency, Def1–Def4, Def7, Def8, or any arithmetic/provability-predicate structure) are assumed.
